    'LEGO gifts for teens (ages 13–16)' => '',
    'LEGO gift ideas for teens (13–16) - BrickAtlas' => '',
    'Hand-picked LEGO sets for teenagers aged 13 to 16, grouped by interest: Star Wars, cars, video games and display models, with tips on choosing the right one.' => '',
    '{n, plural, =1{# set} other{# sets}} picked for their interests, not just the age on the box. All of them are in the current LEGO range.' => '',
    'How to choose LEGO for a teenager' => '',
    'Start with their interests, not their age.' => '',
    'A teenager will be happier with a Batmobile or a console from a favourite game than with an "ages 14+" set that has nothing to do with what they like.' => '',
    '18+ does not mean too hard.' => '',
    'It usually means a longer build and a model meant for display. Anyone who has built LEGO before will manage.' => '',
    'Piece count is build time.' => '',
    '300–500 pieces is one evening, 700–1,000 a few evenings, and over 1,500 is a project for several days of the holiday break.' => '',
    'To play with or to display?' => '',
    'Younger teens more often play with vehicles and minifigures; older ones prefer a model for the desk or shelf.' => '',
    'For the Star Wars fan' => '',
    'A safe choice if they watch "The Mandalorian" or the other series set in that galaxy.' => '',
    'The Mandalorian\'s ship from the series most teens know. Over 900 pieces, yet still sturdy enough to play with, not only to display.' => '',
    'An Imperial Remnant shuttle from "The Mandalorian", rated 14+. A longer, calmer build for someone who likes detail.' => '',
    'The biggest of the three: 1,683 pieces, the Jawa sandcrawler and the Mudhorn from "The Mandalorian". A good pick for the main present.' => '',
    'For the car fan' => '',
    'From small display cars to Technic mechanics with working parts.' => '',
    'Technic means mechanics instead of studs. A supercar model with moving parts and about 900 pieces.' => '',
    'The Batmobile from Christopher Nolan\'s trilogy. It covers two passions at once: cars and comics.' => '',
    'Speed Champions are small, detailed cars. With 358 pieces this one works as an extra or a smaller present.' => '',
    'For the gamer' => '',
    'Sets from games teenagers know: Nintendo, Minecraft and Sonic.' => '',
    'A replica of the console from the 18+ range that a teenager will easily build. Great on a desk, especially for a retro gaming fan.' => '',
    'The Ender Dragon from Minecraft: recognisable to every player and big enough to last longer than one evening.' => '',
    'A big truck from the Sonic range with several characters. More for play than display, good for the younger end of this age group.' => '',
    'For the shelf: ambitious builds' => '',
    'Models that stay on display after the build instead of going back in the box.' => '',
    'A book nook that slots between books on a shelf, with Dumbledore\'s office. A great present for a Harry Potter fan who reads.' => '',
    'The New York skyline from the Architecture range. A smart desk model for someone into travel or design.' => '',
    'A set from the film "Avengers: Doomsday". A fresh release with almost 1,000 pieces.' => '',
    'Can a 14-year-old build an 18+ set?' => '',
    'Yes, if they enjoy building and have some patience. LEGO instructions are picture-based and step by step, and the 18+ label is mostly about the theme and the length of the build.' => '',
    'How much should I spend on a LEGO gift for a teenager?' => '',
    'Sets with 300 to 1,000 pieces are a good starting point. Smaller ones work as an extra, bigger ones as the main present.' => '',
    'What if the set sells out before Christmas?' => '',
    'Every set page shows whether its retirement is coming up. Popular models are worth buying early.' => '',
